'\" t
.\" man/man7/treesitter.7cr
.\" Generated by scripts/man.cr from README.md and the doc comments; regenerate instead of editing.
.TH "treesitter" "7cr" "2026-11-03" "treesitter 0.1.0" "treesitter API Reference"
.SH "NAME"
treesitter \- Crystal bindings to the Tree\-sitter C library: grammars loaded from shared libraries at run time, incremental parsing from a string or a chunked reader, syntax nodes, and queries with the common predicates.
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Crystal bindings to the Tree\-sitter <https://tree\-sitter.github.io> C library: grammars loaded from shared libraries at run time, incremental parsing from a string or a chunked reader, syntax nodes, and queries with the common predicates.
.SH
REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Crystal 1.21 or newer
.IP \(bu 2
libtree\-sitter 0.25 or newer, found through \fB\%pkg\-config\fR (Fedora: \fB\%tree\-sitter\-devel\fR; or build it from the repository <https://github.com/tree\-sitter/tree\-sitter> with \fB\%make \%&& \%sudo \%make \%install\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
Grammars as shared libraries, for example the ones nvim\-treesitter installs, or built with \fB\%tree\-sitter \%build\fR
.SH
INSTALLATION
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
dependencies:
  treesitter:
    github: shpeckman/treesitter
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
require "treesitter"
.EE
.RE
.SH
USAGE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
json   = TreeSitter::Language.load("/usr/lib64/libtree\-sitter\-json.so", "json")
parser = TreeSitter::Parser.new(json)
text   = %({"a": [1, 2]})
tree   = parser.parse(text)

root = tree.root
root.type                                  # => "document"
pair = root.named_child(0).not_nil!.named_child(0).not_nil!
pair.field("key").not_nil!.range           # => 1...4
pair.field("value").not_nil!.start_point   # => TreeSitter::Point.new(0, 6)
.EE
.RE
.SS
Languages
.PP
\fB\%Language.load(name)\fR finds and loads a grammar from \fB\%Language.directories\fR. Lookup checks each directory in order for \fB\%libtree\-sitter\-<name>.so\fR and \fB\%<name>.so\fR; \fB\%Language.find(name)\fR returns the matching path or \fB\%nil\fR. The default directories are \fB\%\(ti/.local/lib\fR, \fB\%/usr/local/lib\fR, \fB\%/usr/local/lib64\fR, \fB\%/usr/lib\fR, and \fB\%/usr/lib64\fR. Replace, append to, or prepend to the array to configure lookup:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
TreeSitter::Language.directories.unshift("/opt/tree\-sitter")
json = TreeSitter::Language.load("json")
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB\%Language.load(name)\fR raises \fB\%TreeSitter::Error\fR when no library is found. \fB\%Language.load(path, \%name, \%symbol \%= \%"tree_sitter_<name>")\fR opens an explicit grammar library and calls its entry function; dashes in the name become underscores in the default symbol. Both forms raise \fB\%TreeSitter::Error\fR when the library or symbol is missing, or when the grammar\(aqs ABI is outside \fB\%Language::MIN_ABI..Language::MAX_ABI\fR (13 to 15). A grammar linked into the program can be wrapped directly: \fB\%Language.new(tree_sitter_json(), \%"json")\fR. Loaded libraries stay open for the life of the process.
.SS
Parsing
.PP
.TS
tab(	);
l lx.
T{
\fBCall\fR
T}	T{
\fBParses\fR
T}
_
T{
\fB\%parse(text, \%old_tree \%= \%nil)\fR
T}	T{
A string
T}
T{
\fB\%parse(old_tree \%= \%nil) \%{ \%|byte| \%bytes \%}\fR
T}	T{
Text read in chunks: the block gets a byte offset and returns the bytes from there, of any length, and an empty slice at the end
T}
T{
\fB\%parse(old_tree, \%cancel: \%\->(byte \%: \%Int32) \%{ \%\[u2026] \%}) \%{ \%|byte| \%bytes \%}\fR
T}	T{
Chunks as above, asking \fB\%cancel\fR with the byte reached as it goes; returns \fB\%nil\fR once it answers \fB\%true\fR (a time budget, a newer edit), and the next parse starts over
T}
.TE
.PP
A chunk must stay unchanged until the next call; the parser keeps the last one alive. Raises \fB\%Error\fR when the parse does not finish.
.PP
To reparse after a change, describe it with \fB\%Tree#edit\fR and pass the old tree:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
tree = parser.parse("[1, 2]")
tree.edit(TreeSitter::Edit.new(start_byte: 5, old_end_byte: 5, new_end_byte: 8,
  start_point: TreeSitter::Point.new(0, 5), old_end_point: TreeSitter::Point.new(0, 5),
  new_end_point: TreeSitter::Point.new(0, 8)))
tree = parser.parse("[1, 2, 3]", tree)
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB\%new_tree.changed_regions(old_tree)\fR lists the \fB\%Region\fRs (bytes and points) whose syntax differs between the edited
old tree and the new one; text edited inside an unchanged structure is not among them.
.PP
\fB\%parser.included_regions \%= \%regions\fR limits the following parses to those regions of the text, in order and not
overlapping, for a language embedded in another one (a \fB\%<script>\fR in HTML, a fenced block in Markdown); an empty array
parses the whole text again. \fB\%Node#region\fR gives a node\(aqs region.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
parser.included_regions = [script.region]
inner = parser.parse(text)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Offsets are bytes into UTF\-8 text. A \fB\%Point\fR is a zero\-based row and a byte column. \fB\%Tree#copy\fR makes an independent copy, cheap to make, for use on another fiber.
.SS
Nodes
.PP
\fB\%Node\fR is a value tied to its \fB\%Tree\fR, and it keeps the tree alive. Every navigation method returns \fB\%nil\fR where there is no node.
.PP
.TS
tab(	);
l lx.
T{
\fBGroup\fR
T}	T{
\fBMethods\fR
T}
_
T{
Identity
T}	T{
\fB\%type\fR, \fB\%named?\fR, \fB\%extra?\fR, \fB\%==\fR
T}
T{
Position
T}	T{
\fB\%start_byte\fR, \fB\%end_byte\fR, \fB\%range\fR, \fB\%start_point\fR, \fB\%end_point\fR
T}
T{
Errors
T}	T{
\fB\%error?\fR (an \fB\%ERROR\fR node), \fB\%missing?\fR (inserted by recovery, zero width), \fB\%has_error?\fR (either one inside)
T}
T{
Tree
T}	T{
\fB\%parent\fR, \fB\%child(i)\fR, \fB\%child_count\fR, \fB\%named_child(i)\fR, \fB\%named_child_count\fR, \fB\%field(name)\fR
T}
T{
Siblings
T}	T{
\fB\%next_sibling\fR, \fB\%previous_sibling\fR, \fB\%next_named_sibling\fR, \fB\%previous_named_sibling\fR
T}
T{
Search
T}	T{
\fB\%descendant(from, \%to)\fR, \fB\%named_descendant(from, \%to)\fR: the smallest node spanning a byte range
T}
.TE
.SS
Queries
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
query = TreeSitter::Query.new(json, <<\-SCM)
  (pair key: (string) @key)
  ((string (string_content) @keyword) (#any\-of? @keyword "if" "else"))
  SCM

query.each_capture(root, text) do |capture|
  capture.name             # "key"
  capture.node.range       # bytes
  capture.pattern          # index of the pattern that matched
end

query.each_match(root, text, 0...40) do |match|
  match["key"]             # first node captured as key
  match.nodes("key")       # every one, for quantified captures
end
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB\%each_capture\fR yields captures in text order, which is what highlighting needs. \fB\%each_match\fR yields whole matches. Both take an optional byte range. The text for predicates comes from a \fB\%TreeSitter::Source\fR (\fB\%text(from, \%to)\fR); passing a \fB\%String\fR wraps it in a \fB\%StringSource\fR. Text held elsewhere, such as a line buffer, includes \fB\%Source\fR so a query never builds the whole document.
.PP
A query the grammar rejects raises \fB\%QueryError\fR, which carries its \fB\%kind\fR (\fB\%Syntax\fR, \fB\%NodeType\fR, \fB\%Field\fR, \fB\%Capture\fR, \fB\%Structure\fR, \fB\%Language\fR) and byte \fB\%offset\fR.
.SS
Predicates
.PP
.TS
tab(	);
l lx.
T{
\fBPredicate\fR
T}	T{
\fBPasses when the capture\(aqs text\fR
T}
_
T{
\fB\%#eq? \%@a \%"x"\fR, \fB\%#eq? \%@a \%@b\fR
T}	T{
Equals the string, or the other capture\(aqs text
T}
T{
\fB\%#match? \%@a \%"re"\fR (also \fB\%#vim\-match?\fR)
T}	T{
Matches the regular expression (PCRE)
T}
T{
\fB\%#lua\-match? \%@a \%"%u%w*"\fR
T}	T{
Matches the Lua pattern, translated to a regular expression
T}
T{
\fB\%#any\-of? \%@a \%"x" \%"y"\fR
T}	T{
Is one of the strings
T}
.TE
.PP
Each predicate has a \fB\%not\-\fR form (\fB\%#not\-eq?\fR, \[u2026]). A capture missing from the match passes. Directives (\fB\%#set!\fR, \fB\%#make\-range!\fR), unknown predicates and a \fB\%#match?\fR whose expression does not compile are skipped, so the match is reported as if they were absent.
.PP
\fB\%query.properties(pattern)\fR holds the \fB\%#set!\fR directives of a pattern as key \[u2192] value (\fB\%""\fR when a key is set without
one): \fB\%(#set! \%injection.language \%"lua")\fR gives \fB\%{"injection.language" \%=> \%"lua"}\fR. Read it with \fB\%match.pattern\fR or
\fB\%capture.pattern\fR.
.SH
SPEC FIXTURE
.PP
\fB\%require \%"treesitter/fixture"\fR (not loaded by \fB\%require \%"treesitter"\fR) adds \fB\%TreeSitter::Fixture\fR. It compiles the bundled JSON grammar (\fB\%fixtures/json\fR, MIT, from tree\-sitter\-json) with \fB\%cc\fR into a temporary directory on first use:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
json = TreeSitter::Fixture.language("json")
TreeSitter::Fixture.library("json")   # path of the compiled .so
TreeSitter::Fixture.clean             # removes the compiled libraries
.EE
.RE
.SH
MAN PAGES
.PP
\fB\%man/\fR holds man pages generated from this README and the API doc comments: \fB\%treesitter(7cr)\fR for the overview and one section 3 page per type, such as \fB\%TreeSitter::Node(3cr)\fR. When the shard is installed as a dependency, its \fB\%postinstall\fR script copies them to \fB\%$XDG_DATA_HOME/man\fR (by default \fB\%\(ti/.local/share/man\fR). Add that directory to \fB\%MANPATH\fR if \fB\%man\fR does not search it.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
man TreeSitter::Parser
man \-l lib/treesitter/man/man3/TreeSitter::Parser.3cr   # without installing
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB\%scripts/man.cr\fR manages the pages and runs from any directory:
.PP
.TS
tab(	);
l lx.
T{
\fBCommand\fR
T}	T{
\fBDoes\fR
T}
_
T{
\fB\%crystal \%run \%scripts/man.cr \%\-\- \%generate\fR
T}	T{
Writes the pages into \fB\%man/\fR from \fB\%crystal \%docs \%\-\-format \%json\fR; unchanged pages keep their file and date, pages of removed types are deleted
T}
T{
\fB\%crystal \%run \%scripts/man.cr \%\-\- \%install\fR
T}	T{
Copies the pages to the manual directory
T}
T{
\fB\%crystal \%run \%scripts/man.cr \%\-\- \%uninstall\fR
T}	T{
Removes those pages from the manual directory
T}
.TE
.PP
\fB\%\-o \%DIR\fR sets the pages directory (relative to the shard root, default \fB\%man\fR) and \fB\%\-d \%DIR\fR the manual directory. \fB\%SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH\fR sets the date of rewritten pages. From a project that depends on the shard, run \fB\%crystal \%run \%lib/treesitter/scripts/man.cr \%\-\- \%uninstall\fR to remove the installed pages.
.SH
LICENSE
.PP
MIT. The bundled JSON grammar keeps its own MIT license in \fB\%fixtures/json/LICENSE\fR.
.SH "TYPES"
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter" (3cr)
Bindings to the Tree\-sitter C library (0.25 or newer, linked through \fB\%pkg\-config\fR): grammars loaded from shared libraries at run time (\fB\%Language.load\fR), incremental parsing (\fB\%Parser\fR, \fB\%Tree#edit\fR), syntax nodes (\fB\%Node\fR) and queries with the common predicates (\fB\%Query\fR).
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Capture" (3cr)
One captured node of a query match: the node, its capture \fB\%id\fR and \fB\%name\fR (\fB\%"keyword"\fR, \fB\%"function.outer"\fR) and the index of the \fB\%pattern\fR that matched.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Edit" (3cr)
One text replacement, given to \fB\%Tree#edit\fR before reparsing: where it starts, where the replaced text ended and where the new text ends, as bytes and as points.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Error" (3cr)
Raised when a grammar cannot be loaded, is of an unsupported ABI version, or a parse cannot start.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Fixture" (3cr)
Grammars bundled for specs (\fB\%require \%"treesitter/fixture"\fR), compiled with \fB\%cc\fR into shared libraries in a temporary directory on first use.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Language" (3cr)
A grammar: the parse tables a \fB\%Parser\fR uses.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Match" (3cr)
One match of a query pattern with all of its captures.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Node" (3cr)
A node of a \fB\%Tree\fR: its type, byte range, points and neighbors.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Parser" (3cr)
Parses UTF\-8 text with one \fB\%Language\fR into a \fB\%Tree\fR.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Point" (3cr)
A position in the text: a zero\-based row and a byte column within it.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Predicate" (3cr)
A text test on a query pattern, checked against the captured nodes\(aq text before a match is reported: \fB\%#eq?\fR, \fB\%#match?\fR, \fB\%#any\-of?\fR, their \fB\%not\-\fR forms and the \fB\%vim\-\fR and \fB\%lua\-\fR spellings of \fB\%#match?\fR.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Predicate::Test" (3cr)
What a predicate compares.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Query" (3cr)
A compiled query (S\-expression patterns with \fB\%@captures\fR and predicates) for one \fB\%Language\fR.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::QueryError" (3cr)
Raised by \fB\%Query.new\fR for a query the grammar rejects, with the byte \fB\%offset\fR into the source and the \fB\%kind\fR of problem.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::QueryError::Kind" (3cr)
What is wrong with the query at \fB\%offset\fR.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Region" (3cr)
A stretch of text as bytes and points: what \fB\%Tree#changed_regions\fR reports and \fB\%Parser#included_regions=\fR takes.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Source" (3cr)
Where a \fB\%Query\fR reads node text for its predicates: anything that returns the text between two byte offsets.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::StringSource" (3cr)
A \fB\%Source\fR over one string.
.TP
.BR "TreeSitter::Tree" (3cr)
A syntax tree made by \fB\%Parser#parse\fR.
